Moisture, grain direction and wood property evidence (@mat-wood-moisture: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @materials-engineering, #source "../sources/Wood-Handbook-FPL-2021.lingua#r_2SP2KMHCD78MQGZN69GKPGJ3WM", #source "../sources/Material-Families-and-Mechanical-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_79PNN694CQ82ENKZC6STQJ5KN9", #materials-engineering "../mechanical/Measure-response-before-selecting-a-material-law.lingua#r_4R1J7QDQJB51X68DBRVHDCE2F6") { r_4R9G8XNCS0G4H4FD4T87S8GPG7
**Read:** [Wood Handbook — Forest Products Laboratory, 2021](../sources/Wood-Handbook-FPL-2021.lingua#r_2SP2KMHCD78MQGZN69GKPGJ3WM), chapters 4–7 and 10–13; [Material families and mechanical response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Material-Families-and-Mechanical-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_79PNN694CQ82ENKZC6STQJ5KN9), Wood's moisture experiment and its stated specimen conditions.

On the usual dry-mass basis, moisture content is 100(m_wet−m_dry)/m_dry percent. A 120 g specimen that dries to 100 g has 20% moisture content on that basis, not 16.7%. Bound water in cell walls and free water in cavities have different relations to swelling and mechanical response. Sorption history, temperature, species and equilibration matter.

Distinguish longitudinal, radial and tangential response and the effects of knots, grain deviation and checks. Moisture cycling under load can change deformation beyond a simple dry-versus-wet modulus comparison. Adhesive performance and differential movement also matter in engineered wood.

**Work:** Reconstruct a documented moisture/property comparison with its mass basis, conditioning and geometry. Explain how a clear-wood test relates to a graded product without substituting its mean strength for a design value. Propose measurements separating moisture redistribution, elastic strain, creep and damage.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
